            <h2>What is mixing?</h2>
            <p>After you finish recording, the raw tracks don't sound like a finished song. The drums might be overwhelming, the vocals too quiet, two guitars muddying each other. Mixing is the process that fixes this.</p>
            <p>A mixing engineer opens all your individual tracks in a DAW and works through:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Levels</strong> — balancing each instrument so nothing dominates or disappears</li>
              <li><strong>Panning</strong> — placing instruments across the stereo field so they have space</li>
              <li><strong>EQ</strong> — cutting unwanted frequencies, boosting the characteristics that make each instrument clear</li>
              <li><strong>Compression</strong> — controlling dynamics so performances feel consistent and controlled</li>
              <li><strong>Effects</strong> — adding reverb, delay, chorus, or other treatments that serve the song</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The result of mixing is a single stereo mix file where all your tracks sound like one cohesive song. Before mixing, raw recorded tracks sound dry, imbalanced, and disconnected from each other.</p>
            <p>Thunder Studio's <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/control-room`}>Control Room</a> is set up for professional mixing with a mixing console, calibrated studio monitors, and a full DAW workstation.</p>

            <h2>What is mastering?</h2>
            <p>Mastering is the last step before you release music. It works on the complete stereo mix that came out of mixing — not on individual tracks.</p>
            <p>A mastering engineer will:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Set loudness</strong> to match streaming platform standards (Spotify targets around -14 LUFS)</li>
              <li><strong>Ensure consistency</strong> across an album so all songs feel equally loud and tonally matched</li>
              <li><strong>Check across multiple playback systems</strong> so the song sounds good on Bluetooth speakers, headphones, and in a car</li>
              <li><strong>Prepare the correct formats</strong> for different platforms: 16-bit WAV for streaming delivery, MP3 for web use</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The key difference from mixing: mastering does not touch individual instrument tracks. It works on the full mix as a single file — like a final polish before delivery.</p>

