            <h2>The recording-to-release workflow</h2>
            <p>Post-production follows a fixed sequence. Each step depends on the one before it:</p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2 text-[1rem] leading-[1.85] text-[rgba(15,14,14,0.75)]">
              <li><strong>Recording (Tracking)</strong> — capture each instrument on separate tracks</li>
              <li><strong>Editing</strong> — remove unwanted sounds, fix timing issues, cut noise between takes</li>
              <li><strong>Mixing</strong> — balance all tracks, apply EQ, compression, and effects</li>
              <li><strong>Mastering</strong> — polish the complete mix, set loudness, prepare formats</li>
              <li><strong>Export for release</strong> — 16-bit 44.1kHz WAV for streaming delivery, or to spec for each platform</li>
            </ol>
            <p>For everything you need before entering the studio, read the <a href={`/${lang}/blog/recording-studio-guide`}>first-time recording guide</a>.</p>

            <h2>What to prepare for a mixing session</h2>
            <p>Before a mixing session at Thunder Studio or before sending files to an engineer, gather the following:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Separate stems</strong> — 24-bit WAV files for each instrument, exported individually. No master bus compression, no normalization, no limiter on the stereo output.</li>
              <li><strong>Reference tracks</strong> — 2-3 songs that sound like what you want your finished mix to sound like. The more specific the better: "I want the drums from this track but the guitar tone from this one."</li>
              <li><strong>Notes on what you want</strong> — tell the engineer the feel you're going for, which instruments to prioritize, where you want the vocals to sit in the mix</li>
              <li><strong>Tempo and time signature</strong> — note them in the session file or a separate text document</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Common mistakes musicians make</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Skipping mastering</strong> — a track without mastering will sound quieter than everything else in a playlist and will not translate well across different playback systems</li>
              <li><strong>Mixing on headphones or laptop speakers</strong> — consumer headphones often exaggerate or cut bass, and laptop speakers remove low end almost entirely. A mix that sounds right on headphones frequently sounds wrong on actual speakers</li>
              <li><strong>Not providing reference tracks</strong> — without references, the engineer has to guess what you want. This leads to multiple revision rounds that slow everything down</li>
              <li><strong>Expecting mixing to fix a bad recording</strong> — mixing can balance levels and add polish, but it cannot fix out-of-tune performances, loose timing, or poor room acoustics. Bad recordings need to be re-tracked.</li>
              <li><strong>Sending heavily compressed or clipped stems</strong> — if your tracks are already slammed and the waveform is flat, the engineer has no dynamic range to work with. Leave headroom in your exports.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
            <p><strong>What is the difference between mixing and mastering?</strong><br />Mixing balances individual tracks. Mastering polishes the final stereo mix. They are separate steps done on different file types, in sequence.</p>
            <p><strong>Can I skip mastering?</strong><br />You can, but your track will sound quieter than others on streaming playlists and won't translate consistently across speakers and headphones. Mastering takes relatively little time and makes a meaningful difference.</p>
            <p><strong>What files do I need to send for mixing?</strong><br />Individual stems as 24-bit WAV files. No master bus compression, no normalization, no limiter. Include tempo and time signature. The cleaner the stems, the better the result.</p>
            <p><strong>How long does mixing take per song?</strong><br />A typical rock song with 8-16 tracks takes 4-8 hours to mix, depending on complexity and how many revisions are needed. Mastering usually takes 1-2 hours per track.</p>
            <p><strong>Can I mix on my laptop myself?</strong><br />You can, but laptop speakers and consumer headphones give a distorted picture of the sound. Mixing on flat-response studio monitors in an acoustically treated room produces far more accurate and consistent results.</p>
            <p><strong>How much does mixing and mastering cost at Thunder Studio?</strong><br />Contact us at 063-546-1924 or LINE @thunderstudio for rates. Pricing depends on the number of tracks and revisions required.</p>

